Because in React, **a child component normally re-renders whenever its parent re-renders**. Even if the props “look” identical, React doesn’t automatically skip calling the child component.

If you’re passing an object literal like this:

```jsx
<Child options={{ color: "red", size: "large" }} />
```

a **new object is created on every parent render**.

So even though it looks the same:

```js
{ color: "red", size: "large" }
```

it is not the same object by reference:

```js
{ color: "red" } === { color: "red" } // false
```

React compares props by reference in many optimization cases, such as `React.memo`.

---

## Example

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <Child options={{ color: "red" }} />
    </>
  );
}

function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div>{options.color}</div>;
}
```

Every time `Parent` renders, this creates a new object:

```jsx
{ color: "red" }
```

So `Child` re-renders too.

---

## Use `React.memo`

You can wrap the child:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div>{options.color}</div>;
});
```

But this alone will **not help** if you still pass a new object literal each time.

```jsx
<Child options={{ color: "red" }} />
```

The `options` prop is still different by reference on every render.

---

## Fix with `useMemo`

Memoize the object in the parent:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const options = useMemo(() => {
    return { color: "red" };
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <Child options={options} />
    </>
  );
}

const Child = React.memo(function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div>{options.color}</div>;
});
```

Now `options` keeps the same reference between renders, so `React.memo` can skip re-rendering `Child`.

---

## If the object depends on values

```jsx
const options = useMemo(() => {
  return {
    color,
    size,
  };
}, [color, size]);
```

Then the object only changes when `color` or `size` changes.

---

## Alternative: pass primitive props

Instead of passing an object:

```jsx
<Child options={{ color: "red", size: "large" }} />
```

you can pass primitives:

```jsx
<Child color="red" size="large" />
```

Then `React.memo` can compare them more easily:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ color, size }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {color} {size}
    </div>
  );
});
```

---

## Important note

A “re-render” does not always mean React updates the DOM. React may call your component again, compare the result, and avoid changing the actual DOM if nothing changed.

But if you want to prevent the child component function from being called unnecessarily, use:

```jsx
React.memo
```

plus stable prop references, usually via:

```jsx
useMemo
```

for objects/arrays, and:

```jsx
useCallback
```

for functions.

---

In short: your object literal creates a new object every render, so the props are not identical by reference. Use `React.memo` and memoize the object with `useMemo`, or pass primitive props instead.

