    function ChildComponent(props) { // No need for React.memo here if parent stabilizes prop
      console.log("Child rendered with props:", props.data);
      return <div>{props.data.name}</div>;
    }

    function ParentComponent({ someOtherState }) { // Assume someOtherState can change
      console.log("Parent rendered");

      // Use useMemo to memoize the object creation.
      // The object will only be recreated if 'someOtherState' changes.
      const myObjectProp = useMemo(() => {
        console.log("Creating new object for myObjectProp...");
        return {
          id: 1,
          name: "Example Item",
          isActive: true
        };
      }, [someOtherState]); // Dependency array: re-create only when someOtherState changes

      return (
        <ChildComponent data={myObjectProp} />
      );
    }
    ```
    In this scenario, if `ParentComponent` re-renders but `someOtherState` hasn't changed, `useMemo` will return the *same* object reference it returned last time. This stable reference is then passed to `ChildComponent`, and if `ChildComponent` is *not* memoized, it will still re-render because its parent re-rendered.

---

**The Best Approach:**

Often, the most robust solution is to combine both:

*   **Use `React.memo` on your child component.** This acts as a safety net.
*   **Use `useMemo` in the parent component** to stabilize the creation of object/array/function props that are expensive to create or whose content doesn't change on every parent re-render.

This ensures that the child component only re-renders when its props *truly* change (either the reference or the underlying data if you're using `useMemo` correctly) and that the parent isn't unnecessarily recreating these props.

The short answer is **referential equality**. 

In JavaScript, objects (including arrays and functions) are compared by their **reference in memory**, not by their actual contents. 

When you pass an object literal like `user={{ name: 'John' }}` to a child component, the parent creates a **brand new object in memory** every single time it renders. Even though the new object *looks* exactly like the old one, React sees a new memory address, assumes the prop has changed, and triggers a re-render.

Here is a quick JavaScript example of how React sees your object:
```javascript
const oldProp = { name: 'John' };
const newProp = { name: 'John' };

console.log(oldProp === newProp); // false!
```

### The Prerequisite: `React.memo`
First, it is important to note that **by default, React components always re-render when their parent re-renders**, regardless of props. 

If you want a component to skip re-rendering when its props haven't changed, you must wrap the child component in `React.memo`:
```javascript
const Child = React.memo(({ myObject }) => {
  return <div>{myObject.name}</div>;
});
```
However, as you discovered, `React.memo` won't work if you pass an object literal, because `React.memo` uses `===` to compare the old props to the new props.

---

### How to fix it

Here are the three best ways to solve this problem, depending on your use case:

#### 1. Memoize the object with `useMemo` (Best for dynamic data)
If the object relies on state or props inside the parent component, use the `useMemo` hook. This tells React to keep the exact same memory reference unless the variables in the dependency array change.

```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // React will reuse the same object reference on every render
  const myObject = useMemo(() => {
    return { name: 'John', id: count };
  }, [count]); // Only creates a new object if 'count' changes

  return <Child myObject={myObject} />;
}
```

#### 2. Move the object outside the component (Best for static data)
If the object never changes and doesn't rely on any state or props, just define it outside of the parent component. Because it's defined outside, it is only created once when the file loads.

```javascript
// Created once in memory
const MY_STATIC_OBJECT = { name: 'John' };

function Parent() {
  return <Child myObject={MY_STATIC_OBJECT} />;
}
```

#### 3. Pass primitive values instead of objects (Simplest)
Strings, numbers, and booleans are passed by **value**, not by reference. `"John" === "John"` is always `true`. If your object only has a few properties, it's often better to just pass them as individual props.

```javascript
function Parent() {
  // Instead of passing user={{ name: 'John', age: 30 }}
  return <Child name="John" age={30} />;
}
```

### Summary
If you are passing an object literal `{}`, an array literal `[]`, or an inline function `() => {}` directly into a component's props, you are breaking `React.memo` and causing unnecessary re-renders. Use `useMemo` (for objects/arrays) or `useCallback` (for functions) to preserve the memory reference!

